+; up to $35,750 if 60–63) Employer contribution Required every year: a match of up to 3% (as low as 1% in up to 2 of 5 years with notice) or a 2% nonelective contribution for all eligible employees Optional — most employers match, but it isn't legally required Setup and admin cost Low — no annual Form 5500 filing, no nondiscrimination testing Higher — annual Form 5500, plan document, possible nondiscrimination testing Roth option Yes, at participating providers (added by SECURE 2.0) Yes, widely available as a Roth 401(k) Plan loans Not allowed Allowed if the plan document permits them Vesting on employer contributions Immediate — 100% vested from day one Can vest over up to 6 years (employer match only) Early withdrawal tax before 59½ 10%, but 25% in the first 2 years of participation 10%, with an exception for leaving the sponsoring employer in or after the year you turn 55 Best for Businesses with 100 or fewer employees wanting minimal admin work Businesses that want higher savings caps and can absorb the admin cost 📣 Building in finance? Put your product in front of readers comparing options on this page. Advertise With Us → Which should you choose? Choose a SIMPLE IRA if you have 100 or fewer employees, want to avoid the cost and paperwork of a full retirement plan, and can fund the employer contribution it requires every year, either a match of up to 3% of pay or 2% of pay for every eligible employee. Choose a 401(k) if you want employees — including yourself — to save more than SIMPLE IRA's lower cap allows, want the flexibility to skip a match in a lean year, or want features like plan loans and vesting schedules. Once you can absorb the added administration cost, a 401(k) almost always wins on flexibility and on how much your highest earners can defer. How SIMPLE IRA and 401(k) contribution limits compare The gap between these two plans widens every year. For 2026, the IRS set the SIMPLE IRA employee deferral limit at $17,000, with a $4,000 catch-up for participants 50 and older. The 401(k) employee deferral limit is $24,500, with a $32,500 catch-up limit for 50+ and a higher $35,750 catch-up for employees aged 60 to 63 under SECURE 2.0's enhanced catch-up rule. That's roughly 44% more room in a 401(k) at the standard deferral level, and the gap grows further for employees over 50. For an owner or high earner trying to maximize tax-advantaged savings, the 401(k)'s higher ceiling alone can justify the added administrative cost. Withdrawal and Rollover Rules: SIMPLE IRA vs 401(k) According to the IRS , taking money out of a SIMPLE IRA within your first two years of participation costs a 25% additional tax instead of the usual 10% penalty, while early distributions from a 401(k) face the standard 10% penalty alongside an age-55 separation exception. The two-year clock begins on the exact day your employer deposits the first contribution into your SIMPLE IRA. During this initial two-year window, you can only roll over balances tax-free from one SIMPLE IRA to another SIMPLE IRA. The IRS treats any rollover to a 401(k) or a Traditional IRA during this period as an early distribution, which triggers ordinary income tax and the full 25% penalty. Once that two-year period ends, rollover restrictions loosen and the penalty drops to the standard 10% rate. After two years of participation, the IRS allows you to roll over SIMPLE IRA balances tax-free into a 401(k), a Traditional IRA, or another eligible workplace plan. You can also move those funds into a Roth IRA after two years, though you must pay income taxes on any pre-tax dollars you convert. The age-55 separation rule marks another major distinction, allowing penalty-free distributions from a 401(k) if you leave or lose your job in the calendar year you turn 55 or older. That rule applies only to the 401(k) held with the employer you just left, and the IRS does not extend this exception to a SIMPLE IRA or any other IRA. Standard penalty exceptions, including disability and a beneficiary of a deceased owner, apply equally to both plans. For a closer look at how workplace plans differ from an individual account you manage entirely on your own, review our Traditional IRA vs. SIMPLE IRA comparison . That comparison explains how an individual account avoids the mandatory employer contribution structures and the two-year 25% penalty window. Neither plan serves as an effective holding place for money you might need before age 59½, and most employees cannot choose between them because the employer decides which plan to sponsor. For business owners and savers deciding how much to defer, plan selection still hinges on headcount limits, required employer contributions, and administrative costs covered across this guide. A change in IRS rules would change this advice. Who Can Sponsor and Who Must Be Included An employer can sponsor a SIMPLE IRA only if the business had 100 or fewer employees who earned $5,000 or more during the prior calendar year, according to the IRS. That 100-worker cutoff counts every employee on payroll who earned that amount, including those who are not yet eligible to receive plan contributions. If business growth pushes your headcount past 100 qualifying workers, the IRS provides a two-year grace period to maintain the plan or transition to another option. Who must be included follows IRS rules: any employee who earned at least $5,000 in any two prior years and expects to make at least $5,000 in the current year. Employees cannot opt out of plan eligibility, and the employer cannot impose a last-day requirement that denies contributions to workers who leave before year-end. For a business owner, this means you cannot exclude eligible part-time staff who clear that $5,000 mark. When an eligible employee decides not to contribute any salary, the business owes no matching dollars on their behalf. That same employee still receives the 2% nonelective employer contribution if the company selected the flat contribution option instead of a matching schedule. Under the IRS one-plan rule, an employer maintaining a SIMPLE IRA generally cannot sponsor any other retirement plan in the same calendar year. That restriction blocks a business owner from running a SIMPLE IRA and a 401(k) at the same time. A growing business that moves to a 401(k) makes that switch effective January 1. An employee who holds two jobs can participate in a SIMPLE IRA at one employer and a 401(k) at another, though the IRS limits combined employee salary deferrals across all plans to $24,500 for 2026. Before you select a plan structure for your company, pull your prior-year payroll reports and count how many workers made $5,000 or more. The Employer Contribution a SIMPLE IRA Requires Under IRS rules, an employer sponsoring a SIMPLE IRA must contribute every year, choosing between a dollar-for-dollar match up to 3% or a flat 2% nonelective contribution for every eligible employee whether they contribute or not. The employer may lower that match to no less than 1% in no more than two of five years, provided they give advance notice to employees. In practice, this reduction offers a limited way to ease a lean year without letting the employer skip the contribution. In a standard 401(k), employer contributions are optional. While a safe harbor 401(k) requires a set employer contribution, a standard 401(k) never requires the business to match employee deferrals. As a result, a 401(k) employer can skip a match in a lean year, while a SIMPLE IRA employer has to contribute something every year. Administrative burden: why 401(k)s cost more to run SIMPLE IRAs skip most of the compliance machinery that makes 401(k)s expensive. There's no annual Form 5500 filing, no nondiscrimination testing, and no plan document to maintain — the provider handles most of the setup with a short adoption agreement. A 401(k) requires an annual Form 5500 filing, a formal plan document, and — unless you use a safe harbor design — nondiscrimination testing to confirm highly compensated employees aren't disproportionately benefiting. The Department of Labor publishes guidance on the fees and duties that come with running a 401(k), which is worth reading before you commit to the higher administrative load. Many providers now bundle this work into a flat monthly fee, so the true cost gap between the two plans is often smaller than the paperwork difference suggests — get quotes before assuming a 401(k) is out of reach. When to switch from a SIMPLE IRA to a 401(k) SIMPLE IRAs run on the calendar year, and you generally can't terminate one mid-year to start a 401(k) — the switch has to take effect January 1, with employees notified in the prior year. Plan the transition at least a few months ahead of your target start date. The usual triggers to make the switch: you've grown past 100 employees (which disqualifies you from SIMPLE IRA eligibility), your highest earners are maxing out the SIMPLE limit and want more room, or you want plan features — loans, vesting schedules, a wider investment menu — that SIMPLE IRAs don't offer. If you're choosing a 401(k) provider for the first time, see our best 401(k) providers for small business roundup, and check whether your business qualifies for the SECURE 2.0 startup tax credit, which can offset up to $5,000 of setup costs per year for three years. Frequently asked questions Can I have a SIMPLE IRA and a 401(k) at the same time? Generally no. The IRS treats SIMPLE IRAs as an exclusive plan — if you sponsor one, you generally can't also maintain a 401(k) for the same employees in the same calendar year. You have to terminate the SIMPLE IRA, effective January 1, before starting a 401(k). What is the 2026 SIMPLE IRA contribution limit? The 2026 SIMPLE IRA employee deferral limit is $17,000, up from $16,500 in 2025. The catch-up contribution for participants 50 and older is $4,000, and a higher $5,250 catch-up applies to employees aged 60 to 63 under SECURE 2.0. Is a 401(k) always better than a SIMPLE IRA? Not always. A 401(k) offers higher contribution limits and more design flexibility, but it costs more to administer and requires more paperwork. For a small business with 100 or fewer employees that wants the lowest-maintenance option, a SIMPLE IRA is often the better fit despite the lower savings cap. Does a SIMPLE IRA require an employer match? Under IRS rules, an employer must contribute to a SIMPLE IRA each year, but that deposit does not always require a 3% match. Your employer can choose between a dollar-for-dollar match of up to 3% of pay and a flat 2% nonelective contribution for all eligible employees. With advance notice, the company can also lower that match to no less than 1% in up to two out of five years. How do early withdrawal rules differ between a SIMPLE IRA and a 401(k)? Under IRS rules, a SIMPLE IRA charges a 25% early withdrawal penalty during your first two years of participation before dropping to 10%, whereas a 401(k) charges 10% before age 59½. A 401(k) also allows penalty-free withdrawals if you leave your employer at age 55 or older, an exception that does not apply to a SIMPLE IRA. Standard IRS penalty exceptions, such as disability, still apply to early distributions from both a SIMPLE IRA and a 401(k). Who is eligible for a SIMPLE IRA? Under IRS rules, you are eligible for an employer's SIMPLE IRA if you earned at least $5,000 in any two prior years and expect to earn at least $5,000 in the current year. Once you qualify, employees cannot opt out of participation in the SIMPLE IRA. A business qualifies to sponsor a SIMPLE IRA only if it employed 100 or fewer workers who earned at least $5,000 in the preceding year. How do I switch from a SIMPLE IRA to a 401(k)? Notify employees before November 2 of the year prior to the switch, terminate the SIMPLE IRA effective December 31, and start the new 401(k) on January 1. Work with a 401(k) provider several months ahead of the target date to have the plan document and payroll integration ready in time. How does a SIMPLE IRA compare to a SEP-IRA instead of a 401(k)? See our SIMPLE IRA vs. SEP-IRA comparison for the employer-contribution and eligibility differences between those two small-business options. Free calculators to help you decide 401(k) Calculator → Retirement Savings Calculator → Retirement Hub → Sources We prioritize primary sources for rules, formulas, rates, limits, and definitions. See our calculator methodology and editorial policy . IRS — 401(k) Limit Increases to $24,500 for 2026 IRS — SIMPLE IRA Contribution Limits IRS — Retirement Topics: Tax on Early Distributions IRS — Retirement Plans FAQs Regarding SIMPLE IRA Plans DOL — Understanding Your Retirement Plan Fees Related comparisons SEP-IRA vs Solo 401(k) SEP-IRA vs Solo 401(k): 2025 Guide for the Self-Employed 401(k) vs Roth IRA 401(k) vs Roth IRA: Which Retirement Account Wins? Pension vs 401(k) Pension vs 401(k): Key Differences (2025) Solo 401(k) vs SIMPLE IRA Solo 401(k) vs SIMPLE IRA: The 2025 Guide for Owners ModernWallet Free, accurate financial calculators — auto loans, mortgages, retirement, investing, rentals, and net worth. No signup, instant results. 📣 Advertise With Us Calculators All calculators Auto Loan Mortgage Retirement Investing Portfolio Real Estate Net Worth Budget Tax Resolution Estate Planning Probate Elder Care Trump Account 529 College Savings Coast FIRE Business Loan Payoff Personal Loan Interest Per Day Taxable vs. Tax-Deferred Credit Card Payoff IUL Merchant Cash Advance Invoice Factoring Business Line of Credit PTO Cash-Out Zakat Life Insurance Needs Self-Employment Tax Operating Cash Flow Mileage Deduction Cash Conversion Cycle Freelance Rate HELOC S-Corp Tax Commercial Mortgage CD Calculator Emergency Fund Debt Consolidation Inflation Expense Ratio Social Security Break-Even Mileage Reimbursement Portfolio Rebalancing Hurricane Deductible Annuity Credit Card Interest Loan Payment HYSA vs CD Trump Account vs 529 Mortgage Recast Socia